---
import RocmGuide from '../../layouts/RocmGuide.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import Cite from '../../components/rocm/Cite.astro';
import GoDeeper from '../../components/rocm/GoDeeper.astro';
import Sources from '../../components/rocm/Sources.astro';
import type { CiteKey } from '../../lib/rocm-guide';

const used = [
  'sc16Press',
  'sc16PressIr',
  'rocmVersions',
  'theRock',
  'rocm10Notes',
  'rocm101Notes',
  'rocm101Blog',
  'hipWhat',
] as const satisfies readonly CiteKey[];

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: 'how-to-read', text: 'How to read this timeline' },
  { depth: 2, slug: '2016', text: '2016 — the name ROCm' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'table', text: 'Official version table (5.0 → 10.1)' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'therock', text: 'TheRock and the 10.x line' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'open', text: 'What we are not filling in' },
];
---

<RocmGuide page="history" headings={headings}>
  <h2 id="how-to-read">How to read this timeline</h2>
  <p>
    Only dated claims with a primary source go in the table. Marketing slides, wiki reconstructions,
    and “I think 1.0 shipped in April” stay out unless AMD (or a contemporaneous AMD press page)
    says so. Gaps are marked TODO, not guessed.
  </p>

  <h2 id="2016">2016 — the name ROCm</h2>
  <p>
    On <strong>14 November 2016</strong>, at SC16 in Salt Lake City, AMD announced a new release
    of the <strong>Radeon Open Compute Platform (ROCm)</strong>: software support for then-new
    Radeon GPU hardware, new math libraries, and a foundation of programming languages for
    heterogeneous compute. The same release discussed planned OpenCL support and future CPU
    targets (including then-upcoming Zen).<Cite id="sc16Press" used={used} /><Cite id="sc16PressIr" used={used} />
  </p>
  <p>
    That press release is the clean public naming event. HIP — the C++ API still in the stack —
    is documented today as part of ROCm and as a way to port CUDA-like code, not as a drop-in
    CUDA clone.<Cite id="hipWhat" used={used} />
    We do not attach a HIP 0.x tag date here without an AMD primary source.
  </p>

  <Callout type="note" title="TODO: first public 1.0 tag">
    Secondary reporting often says an earlier ROCm 1.0 shipped in 2016 before the SC16 1.3
    announcement. We have not attached an AMD primary URL for that 1.0 date. Until we do, the
    first <em>cited</em> public milestone on this page is the 14 November 2016 press release.
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="table">Official version table (5.0 → 10.1)</h2>
  <p>
    Current AMD docs publish a <a href="https://rocm.docs.amd.com/en/latest/release/versions.html">release history</a>
    that starts at <strong>5.0.0 — 9 February 2022</strong> and runs through
    <strong>10.1.0 — 5 October 2026</strong>.<Cite id="rocmVersions" used={used} />
    Earlier 1.x–4.x numbers are not on that table. Selected rows (full table is at the source):
  </p>
  <div class="card overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="table-lab min-w-[28rem]">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Version</th>
          <th>Date (AMD table)</th>
          <th>Why it is on this page</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>5.0.0</td>
          <td>9 Feb 2022</td>
          <td>Oldest row on the current official history table.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>6.0.0</td>
          <td>15 Dec 2023</td>
          <td>6.x line opens.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>7.0.0</td>
          <td>16 Sep 2025</td>
          <td>7.x line opens.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>7.14.0</td>
          <td>15 Jul 2026</td>
          <td>TheRock becomes the build/release system from 7.14.<Cite id="rocm101Notes" used={used} /></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>7.14.1</td>
          <td>2 Sep 2026</td>
          <td>7.14 patch line (dated after 10.0.0 on the same table).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>10.0.0</td>
          <td>26 Aug 2026</td>
          <td>Production 10.0. R9700 / RX 9000 on the device table.<Cite id="rocm10Notes" used={used} /></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>10.1.0</td>
          <td>5 Oct 2026</td>
          <td>Current notes this guide cites. CLI v0.2.0 in the blog.<Cite id="rocm101Notes" used={used} /><Cite id="rocm101Blog" used={used} /></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <GoDeeper title="Why 7.14.1 is dated after 10.0.0">
    <p>
      The official table lists 10.0.0 on 26 August 2026 and 7.14.1 on 2 September 2026.<Cite id="rocmVersions" used={used} />
      Parallel lines exist. A 7.14.x wheel or image is not “older than 10.0” just because the
      major number is smaller — check the date and the gfx list on the artifact you actually
      installed.
    </p>
  </GoDeeper>

  <h2 id="therock">TheRock and the 10.x line</h2>
  <p>
    ROCm 10.0 and 10.1 notes both state that <strong>since ROCm 7.14</strong>, ROCm uses
    <strong>TheRock</strong> as its build and release system.<Cite id="rocm10Notes" used={used} /><Cite id="rocm101Notes" used={used} />
    The 10.0 docs include a <a href="https://rocm.docs.amd.com/en/docs-10.0.0/about/transition-guide-TheRock.html">transition guide</a>.<Cite id="theRock" used={used} />
    For home users that is the packaging plot twist: <code>amdrocm-*</code> names, scoped
    prefixes under <code>/opt/rocm</code>, and “do not mix archive <code>rocm</code>” as a
    first-week failure mode. Walkthrough: <a href="/tutorials/install-rocm">install</a>. Policy:
    <a href="/rocm/versions">versions</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    10.1’s public narrative (Infinity Storage / hipFILE, NUMA-aware HIP alloc, LLVM 24,
    <code>amd-smi</code>, WSL2 preview, ROCm CLI v0.2.0) is the
    <a href="https://rocm.blogs.amd.com/ecosystems-and-partners/rocm-10.1-blog/README.html">5 October 2026 blog</a>.<Cite id="rocm101Blog" used={used} />
  </p>

  <h2 id="open">What we are not filling in</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>TODO: AMD primary source for the first ROCm 1.0 tag date (often claimed as April 2016).</li>
    <li>TODO: AMD primary source for the SC15 “Boltzmann Initiative” announcement, if we add a 2015 row.</li>
    <li>TODO: first HIP public tag (community cites early 2016 HIP releases; not attached here).</li>
    <li>We do not reconstruct 2.x–4.x from blogs or Wikipedia.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you have an AMD newsroom or docs URL for those rows, they belong in
    <code>src/lib/rocm-guide.ts</code> and then here — not in a footnote we invent.
  </p>

  <Sources used={used} />
</RocmGuide>
